name: dotnet-channels

Guide to `System.Threading.Channels`: thread-safe producer/consumer queues using `Channel<T>`, `ChannelWriter<T>`, and `ChannelReader<T>`. Covers bounded/unbounded creation, backpressure, `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`, scaling consumers, and graceful completion.

Related skills: [skill:dotnet-background-services] for hosted-service integration; [skill:dotnet-csharp-async-patterns] for async/await and cancellation-token propagation.

## Creation and capacity

```csharp
Channel<WorkItem> channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<WorkItem>();
ChannelWriter<WorkItem> writer = channel.Writer;
ChannelReader<WorkItem> reader = channel.Reader;
```

Prefer bounded channels in production; they cap memory and apply backpressure. Unbounded channels grow without limit and can exhaust memory under load; use only for controlled, low-volume producers.

```csharp
var bounded = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(
    new BoundedChannelOptions(capacity: 1000)
    { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait });
var unbounded = Channel.CreateUnbounded<WorkItem>();
```

`FullMode` behavior when full:
- `Wait`: `WriteAsync` waits for space; reliable backpressure (default).
- `DropOldest`: discard oldest; useful when latest telemetry matters.
- `DropNewest`: discard the item being written; rate limiting.
- `DropWrite`: discard the item being written; `TryWrite` returns `false`.

```csharp
var telemetryChannel = Channel.CreateBounded<SensorReading>(
    new BoundedChannelOptions(500) { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropOldest });
var logChannel = Channel.CreateBounded<LogEntry>(
    new BoundedChannelOptions(10_000) { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropWrite });
if (!logChannel.Writer.TryWrite(entry)) { /* track overflow */ }
```

Since .NET 7, drop modes support an `itemDropped` callback for metrics, logging, rerouting, or cleanup:
```csharp
var channel = Channel.CreateBounded(new BoundedChannelOptions(100)
{
    FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropOldest
}, itemDropped: (item, writer) =>
{
    logger.LogWarning("Dropped item due to channel overflow: {Id}", item.Id);
    (item as IDisposable)?.Dispose();
});
```
The callback receives the dropped item and `ChannelWriter<T>`.

## Writing and completion

Use `WriteAsync(item, cancellationToken)` for backpressure; `TryWrite(item)` is non-blocking and returns false if full or completed. Multiple producers may write concurrently without external locking.

Call `Complete()` or `TryComplete()` when no more items will be produced so readers can detect completion. `TryComplete()` is idempotent; pass an exception to propagate failure:
```csharp
writer.TryComplete(new InvalidOperationException("Source failed"));
```

## Reading and processing

Preferred drain pattern: read all available items before awaiting again, reducing async state-machine overhead:
```csharp
while (await reader.WaitToReadAsync(cancellationToken))
    while (reader.TryRead(out var item))
        await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);
```
For simpler cases, use `ReadAsync(cancellationToken)` then process the item.

Scale processing with multiple consumer tasks; each item is read by exactly one consumer. A `BackgroundService` can start workers with `Enumerable.Range(0, WorkerCount)` and await `Task.WhenAll`; each worker drains the reader, creates a DI scope with `scopeFactory.CreateScope()`, handles items, logs processing errors, and observes the stopping token.

`ReadAllAsync(cancellationToken)` returns `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` and is the simplest consumption pattern; it handles waiting/draining and ends after completion and drain:
```csharp
await foreach (var item in reader.ReadAllAsync(cancellationToken))
    await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);
```
It integrates with ASP.NET Core streaming:
```csharp
app.MapGet("/api/events/stream", (
    ChannelReader<ServerEvent> reader, CancellationToken ct) =>
    reader.ReadAllAsync(ct));
```
With the `System.Linq.Async` NuGet package, compose async streams with LINQ operators such as `.Where(...)` and `.Buffer(50)`.

